; CG-10 target fixture: a literal arm whose type differs from the scrutinee.
;
; The scrutinee is a String and the first two arms carry int literals, so those
; arms must fall through and `case _:` must fire. Every other bare-scrutinee
; fixture in the corpus matches on literals of the SAME type as the scrutinee,
; so the "a mismatched literal never fires" half of the rule had no target: a
; mutation that made a mismatched arm fire unconditionally moved no verdict at
; all (measured 2026-09-15 for criteria-gap row CG-10).
;
; Why this fixture and not an inline test: the two interpreter arms share
; RuntimeOps.matchArmMatches, so a mutation inside it moves both arms together
; and any assertion that only compares the two arms stays green. The LLVM arm
; emits its own comparison, so it is the only independent witness — and it only
; runs over on-disk fixtures. Measured before committing: all three channels
; agree on `wild` (5 bytes each).
main|func() -> ():
    match "s":
        case 1:
            print("one")
        case 2:
            print("two")
        case _:
            print("wild")
    return
